---
import { sitePath } from '../../site.config.mjs';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AuthorityDiagram from '../components/AuthorityDiagram.astro';
import BrandBand from '../components/BrandBand.astro';
import RecordBlock from '../components/RecordBlock.astro';
import { version } from '../../source-provenance.json';
---
<Layout home title="Software authority, explicitly asserted, independently evaluated" description="Who stands behind this software? Make clear who is responsible for a release, what they commit to and what you can rely on.">
  <div class="home-cover shell"><BrandBand /></div>
  <section class="hero shell" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Explicit commitments about software</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Who stands behind<br /><em>this software?</em></h1>
      <p class="hero-intro">Code gets copied, adapted and produced by agents. Make clear who stands behind a release, what they undertake, and what you choose to rely on.</p>
      <p class="implementation-note">AUTHORITY.md makes explicit who stands behind which artifacts and on what terms, alongside <a href={sitePath('/related-practices/')}>SECURITY.md policies, SBOM inventories, SLSA provenance, Sigstore signatures and applicable CRA obligations</a>.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="as-button as-button--primary" href={sitePath('/why-now/#what-this-can-change')}>See what could change</a><a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/declaration/')}>Read the declaration</a></div>
      <p class="draft-status"><strong>Draft proposal · AUTHORITY.md {version}.</strong> <a href={sitePath('/about/')}>Read its purpose and limits</a>.</p>
      <p class="implementation-note">Compatible with open-source and enterprise software.<br />Independent of license and business model.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-visual"><AuthorityDiagram /><p class="visual-note">The operator declares. The consumer decides.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="context-band" aria-labelledby="context-title">
    <div class="shell context-inner"><p class="eyebrow">Why this site exists</p><div><h2 id="context-title">Making software and standing<br /> behind it are different acts.</h2><p>Code tells you what can run. A license tells you what is permitted. A release tells you what is available. You still need to know who stands behind these exact bytes, what they claim, and for how long.</p><p>This problem predates AI. Agents add more ways to produce and adapt software, making the distinction more pressing. This site asks what would change if commitments were stated clearly and consumers could explain what they rely on.</p><a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/why-now/#why-this-site-exists')}>Read why this matters</a></div></div>
  </section>
  <section class="principles shell" aria-labelledby="principles-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">What this could change</p><h2 id="principles-title">Fewer assumptions.<br />Clearer commitments.</h2></div>
    <div class="principle-grid">
      <article><span class="small-label">For publishers</span><h3>Say what you<br /> stand behind.</h3><p>Separate producing, reviewing and maintaining a release. State a useful commitment without appearing to promise everything.</p></article>
      <article><span class="small-label">For consumers</span><h3>Decide what<br /> you rely on.</h3><p>Choose which commitments matter for your use. Explain what you rely on, why, and when you will reconsider.</p></article>
      <article><span class="small-label">For maintainers and forks</span><h3>Stand behind<br /> your own work.</h3><p>Make your own contribution and commitments visible. An upstream name need not stand in for responsibility for a different release.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="worked-example shell" aria-labelledby="example-title">
    <div class="example-intro"><p class="eyebrow">A small example</p><h2 id="example-title">Who will maintain<br /><em>this release?</em></h2><p>A fictional maintainer promises security fixes for a specific Linux release through <time data-example-date="commitment">the end of next year</time>. A team considering the release can see what is promised and decide whether that is enough.</p><a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/')}>Follow the worked examples</a><p class="implementation-note">Illustrative dates adjust to the day you read this page.</p></div>
    <div class="claim-sheet">
      <div class="sheet-heading"><strong>example-package · 1.4.2</strong><span>Illustration only</span></div>
      <div class="artifact-line">SOFTWARE <code>Linux release archive</code><span>Same release throughout</span></div>
      <div class="record-stack">
        <RecordBlock kind="statement" who="Example maintainer">
          <p>“We will investigate reported vulnerabilities and provide fixes for confirmed vulnerabilities in this Linux release through <time data-example-date="commitment">the end of next year</time>.”</p>
          <dl class="as-record__meta"><dt>Statement issued</dt><dd><time data-example-date="issued">30 days ago</time></dd><dt>Support promised through</dt><dd><time data-example-date="commitment">the end of next year</time></dd></dl>
        </RecordBlock>
        <RecordBlock kind="finding" who="Example evaluator">
          <p>The promise covers this Linux release. It excludes other platforms, new features and applications that use the package.</p>
          <p class="record-detail">The statement tells us what the maintainer undertakes. We still need evidence that they can carry it out.</p>
        </RecordBlock>
        <RecordBlock kind="decision" who="Example consumer">
          <p><span class="as-record__verdict">Defer</span> Before relying on this promise for our service, we need to check the maintenance history and agree on response times.</p>
        </RecordBlock>
      </div>
      <p class="sheet-note">The maintainer states the commitment. The consumer checks whether it meets their needs. A publisher can also offer <a href={sitePath('/examples/#public-support-with-an-extension-for-one-customer')}>extended support to specific customers</a>.</p>
      <details><summary>Does this guarantee that fixes will arrive?</summary><p>No. A written commitment makes the promise clear; it does not prove that future work will happen. A consumer may also look for a maintenance track record, evidence of available resources or a support agreement.</p></details>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="use-section shell" aria-labelledby="use-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Use the idea</p><h2 id="use-title">Start with one release.<br />Make one commitment clear.</h2><p>You do not need a new story about every line of code.<br />You need a clear account of the artifact in hand.</p></div>
    <div class="use-grid"><a class="use-link" href={sitePath('/use-it/#for-publishers')}><span class="small-label">I publish software</span><h3>Prepare a claim</h3><p>Identify the artifact, scope, evidence and commitments your consumers need.</p><span class="link-label">Read the publisher guide</span></a><a class="use-link" href={sitePath('/use-it/#for-consumers')}><span class="small-label">I consume software</span><h3>Define acceptance</h3><p>Choose the evidence and checks your policy will require. Keep findings separate from decisions.</p><span class="link-label">Read the consumer guide</span></a></div>
    <p class="implementation-note">Start with the <a href={sitePath('/examples/#a-complete-adoption-example')}>complete fictional adoption example</a>. Use the evidence your decision needs, from a support policy to authenticated release records. <a href={sitePath('/related-practices/#why-use-authoritymd-as-well')}>See when AUTHORITY.md adds value alongside SECURITY.md and an SBOM</a>.</p>
    <p class="implementation-note">The aim is a relationship people can inspect, question and revise. <a href={sitePath('/why-now/#what-this-can-change')}>Explore what this could change</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="declaration-banner shell" aria-labelledby="declaration-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">AUTHORITY.md · Declaration version {version}</p><h2 id="declaration-title">Keep the license.<br /><em>Make authority explicit.</em></h2><p>License rights remain unchanged. Trust stays optional and contestable.</p></div><a class="as-button as-button--light" href={sitePath('/declaration/')}>Read the full declaration</a></section>
</Layout>
